\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Experimental Setup}

\paragraph{Model and Predictor.}
We evaluate on LLaMA-3.1-8B~\cite{grattafiori2024llama3}, which uses SwiGLU FFN blocks~\cite{shazeer2020glu} with $d_{\text{model}}=4096$ and $d_{\text{ff}}=14336$ across 32 layers.
The sparsity predictor is a two-layer MLP with 2.37M parameters per layer (75.9M total), implemented as Linear($4096 \to 128$) + GELU + Linear($128 \to 14336$), trained to produce per-neuron importance scores that minimize output-distribution divergence under sparsity.

\paragraph{Training.}
All predictors are trained for 1000 steps on WikiText-103 with sequence length 2048 and batch size 4, using AdamW with learning rate $1 \times 10^{-3}$ and cosine decay.
The KL predictor uses Gumbel-Sigmoid relaxation~\cite{jang2017gumbel} with temperature $\tau=1.0$ annealed to $0.1$ over training.

\paragraph{Evaluation.}
We report perplexity on WikiText-2~\cite{merity2017pointer} (seq\_len=2048) and downstream accuracy on ARC-Challenge (25-shot), WinoGrande (5-shot), MMLU (5-shot), and GSM8K (8-shot).

\paragraph{Inference Protocol.}
At inference, the predictor produces per-neuron scores that are passed to TEAL's global top-$k$ allocation mechanism~\cite{liu2025teal}, which distributes the sparsity budget across layers non-uniformly.
We term this \textit{predictor-TEAL}: the predictor replaces TEAL's magnitude-based scoring while retaining its allocation strategy.

\paragraph{Baselines.}
We compare against: (1) \textbf{Vanilla TEAL}~\cite{liu2025teal}, which uses activation magnitudes directly; (2) \textbf{BCE predictor}, trained with per-neuron binary cross-entropy loss for 1000 and 2700 steps; (3) \textbf{Uniform allocation}, which distributes the sparsity budget equally across layers; and (4) \textbf{WINA}~\cite{liu2025teal}, an importance-weighted variant.


\subsection{Main Results}

Table~\ref{tab:main} presents the primary comparison across sparsity levels.
At 30\% sparsity, KL distillation with global TEAL allocation achieves PPL 8.98, reducing TEAL's degradation from dense (10.97 $\to$ 8.98, an 18\% improvement). % exp_id: kl_30_global_teal
Uniform allocation at 30\% recovers MMLU to 40.61\% (+15.3pp over KL-global's 25.27\%), indicating that at low sparsity, global top-$k$ starves knowledge-critical layers and uniform budget prevents this. % exp_id: bench_kl30_uniform

At 50\% sparsity, KL distillation over vanilla TEAL widens the PPL advantage: 11.79 vs.\ 23.31, a 49\% reduction. % exp_id: bench_kl50_global, bench_teal50
However, all methods exhibit downstream collapse on MMLU (27.89\% vs.\ 27.77\% for global vs.\ uniform, both $\approx$ random) and GSM8K ($<$2.2\%), indicating a capacity-limited regime where no allocation strategy can recover. % exp_id: bench_kl50_global, d030_kl50_uniform

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Main results on LLaMA-3.1-8B. PPL$\downarrow$ on WikiText-2; accuracy$\uparrow$ on downstream tasks. \textbf{Bold}: best among compensation-free methods per sparsity level. BCE uses a different inference protocol (hard mask + compensation network). Multi-seed variance (3 seeds, 50\% uniform): PPL std=0.07; see Appendix~\ref{app:stability}.}
\label{tab:main}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{
\begin{tabular}{llccccc}
\toprule
Sparsity & Method & PPL$\downarrow$ & ARC-c & WG & MMLU & GSM8K \\
\midrule
0\% & Dense & 6.12 & 58.28 & 78.06 & 65.27 & 48.75 \\
\midrule
\multirow{3}{*}{30\%}
& TEAL vanilla & 10.97 & \textbf{42.32} & 66.06 & 38.61 & 7.88 \\ % exp_id: bench_teal30
& KL global (Ours) & 8.98 & 39.68 & \textbf{70.17} & 25.27 & \textbf{8.79} \\ % exp_id: lm_eval_kl_30pct_s42
& KL uniform & \textbf{8.82} & 40.78 & 68.59 & \textbf{40.61} & 7.35 \\ % exp_id: bench_kl30_uniform
\midrule
\multirow{4}{*}{50\%}
& TEAL vanilla & 23.31 & 28.41 & 53.04 & \textbf{28.32} & 1.97 \\ % exp_id: bench_teal50
& KL global (Ours) & \textbf{11.79} & \textbf{32.85} & \textbf{63.46} & 27.89 & 1.29 \\ % exp_id: bench_kl50_global
& KL uniform & 11.96 & 32.76 & 62.90 & 27.77 & \textbf{2.12} \\ % exp_id: d030_kl50_uniform
& BCE (2700 steps)$^\ast$ & 27.97 & 23.98 & 50.59 & 30.02 & 4.85 \\ % exp_id: eval_bce_v3_s42
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}


\subsection{Iso-Compute Analysis}
\label{sec:isocompute}

A natural question is whether BCE training simply requires more compute to converge.
We train the BCE predictor for 2700 steps (2.71$\times$ the KL budget) and evaluate under identical TEAL inference.
As Table~\ref{tab:main} shows, the BCE predictor at 2700 steps achieves PPL 27.97—still 2.37$\times$ worse than KL at 1000 steps (PPL 11.79). % exp_id: bce_isocompute_s42

The failure is structural, not computational.
BCE loss decomposes into independent per-neuron gradients, training each neuron's prediction in isolation.
This factorized objective cannot learn the inter-neuron correlations required for global top-$k$ allocation, where the keep/prune decision for neuron $i$ depends on the scores of all other neurons across all layers.
We observe that BCE training loss saturates early: between steps 500 and 2700, the loss decreases by only 0.006, confirming that additional compute yields diminishing returns under the wrong objective.


\subsection{Ablation Studies}

Table~\ref{tab:ablation} isolates the contribution of each design choice at 50\% sparsity.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Ablation at 50\% sparsity on LLaMA-3.1-8B (WikiText-2 PPL$\downarrow$, unified benchmark protocol).}
\label{tab:ablation}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
Configuration & PPL$\downarrow$ & $\Delta$ from Ours \\
\midrule
KL + Gumbel (Ours) & \textbf{11.79} & --- \\ % exp_id: bench_kl50_global
KL + STE & 15.77 & +3.98 \\ % exp_id: ste_baseline_s42
BCE + Compensation (hard mask) & 25.44 & +13.65 \\ % exp_id: bce_comp_staged_s42
BCE (no compensation) & 27.97 & +16.18 \\ % exp_id: eval_bce_v3_s42
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Gumbel vs.\ STE.}
Replacing Gumbel-Sigmoid relaxation with Straight-Through Estimation~\cite{bengio2013ste} degrades PPL from 11.79 to 15.77 (+3.98). % exp_id: ste_baseline_s42
Gumbel relaxation provides smooth gradients through the mask sampling, accounting for 34.5\% of the total improvement over vanilla TEAL (3.98 out of 11.52 PPL gap).

\paragraph{SPON Contribution.}
Under the same evaluation protocol (\texttt{evaluate.py}), adding SPON~\cite{xu2026spon} bias correction reduces PPL from 12.08 to 11.79, a 2.4\% relative improvement.\footnote{SPON analysis uses \texttt{evaluate.py} protocol; main results (Table~\ref{tab:main}) use the unified benchmark protocol. The coincidental equality of 11.79 in both settings does not imply equivalence.} % exp_id: spon_2048_s42, mvp_kl_norm_v2_s42
We analyze this limited contribution further in \S\ref{sec:spon}.

We analyze this limited contribution further in \S\ref{sec:spon}.

\paragraph{BCE + Compensation.}
Compensation (output rescaling after hard-mask pruning) improves BCE from 27.97 to 25.44 (9.0\% relative), but this hard-mask protocol remains 2.16$\times$ worse than KL under TEAL inference. % exp_id: bce_comp_staged_s42
We report this separately as it uses a different inference protocol (hard mask vs.\ TEAL global allocation).


\subsection{Sparsity Scaling and Regime Analysis}
\label{sec:scaling}

Table~\ref{tab:scaling} and Figure~\ref{fig:sparsity_sweep} show PPL as a function of sparsity for KL-predictor-TEAL and vanilla TEAL.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig2_sparsity_sweep.pdf}
\caption{Perplexity scaling with sparsity. The KL predictor's advantage grows disproportionately as more neurons are pruned.}
\label{fig:sparsity_sweep}
\end{figure}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{PPL scaling with sparsity (WikiText-2). KL advantage grows disproportionately with sparsity.}
\label{tab:scaling}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Sparsity & KL (Ours) & TEAL vanilla & Relative advantage \\
\midrule
30\% & \textbf{8.98} & 10.97 & 18\% \\ % exp_id: kl_30_global_teal
50\% & \textbf{11.79} & 23.31 & 49\% \\ % exp_id: bench_kl50_global
70\% & \textbf{17.91} & 100.11 & 82\% \\ % exp_id: kl_70_global_teal
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The KL predictor's advantage grows from 18\% at 30\% sparsity to 82\% at 70\% sparsity.
At high sparsity, TEAL's magnitude-based scoring collapses (PPL 100.11), while the learned predictor maintains coherent allocation by exploiting input-dependent contextual information.

\paragraph{Three Regimes: Allocation vs.\ Capacity.}
Table~\ref{tab:phase} presents the full allocation matrix: global vs.\ uniform allocation at three sparsity levels, revealing a qualitative regime shift in the failure mode.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Allocation analysis: global (TEAL) vs.\ uniform per-layer allocation across sparsity levels. \textbf{Bold}: best per column within each sparsity group (compensation-free). Each row uses a predictor trained at the respective sparsity target.}
\label{tab:phase}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{
\begin{tabular}{llccccc}
\toprule
Sparsity & Allocation & PPL$\downarrow$ & ARC-c & WG & MMLU & GSM8K \\
\midrule
0\% & Dense & 6.12 & 58.28 & 78.06 & 65.27 & 48.75 \\
\midrule
\multirow{3}{*}{30\%}
& TEAL vanilla & 10.97 & \textbf{42.32} & 66.06 & 38.61 & 7.88 \\ % exp_id: bench_teal30
& KL global & 8.98 & 39.68 & \textbf{70.17} & 25.27 & \textbf{8.79} \\ % exp_id: lm_eval_kl_30pct_s42
& KL uniform & \textbf{8.82} & 40.78 & 68.59 & \textbf{40.61} & 7.35 \\ % exp_id: bench_kl30_uniform
\midrule
\multirow{2}{*}{40\%}
& KL global & 10.12 & 40.96 & 65.35 & \textbf{38.06} & \textbf{5.00} \\ % exp_id: bench_kl40_global
& KL uniform & \textbf{8.36} & \textbf{41.81} & \textbf{67.17} & 30.68 & 3.94 \\ % exp_id: d030_kl40_uniform
\midrule
\multirow{3}{*}{50\%}
& TEAL vanilla & 23.31 & 28.41 & 53.04 & \textbf{28.32} & 1.97 \\ % exp_id: bench_teal50
& KL global & \textbf{11.79} & \textbf{32.85} & \textbf{63.46} & 27.89 & 1.29 \\ % exp_id: bench_kl50_global
& KL uniform & 11.96 & 32.76 & 62.90 & 27.77 & \textbf{2.12} \\ % exp_id: d030_kl50_uniform
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

The allocation preference is \emph{non-monotonic} across sparsity levels, revealing three distinct regimes:

\noindent\textbf{Budget-surplus ($\leq$30\%).}
Uniform allocation recovers MMLU from 25.27\% to 40.61\% (+15.3pp); global top-$k$ starves knowledge-critical layers when the budget is ample. % exp_id: bench_kl30_uniform, lm_eval_kl_30pct_s42

\noindent\textbf{Budget-constrained ($\sim$40\%).}
The preference reverses: global achieves MMLU 38.06\% vs.\ uniform's 30.68\% (+7.4pp), as the predictor's learned priorities outweigh uniform waste. % exp_id: bench_kl40_global, d030_kl40_uniform
PPL remains low (8.36) but downstream accuracy degrades, confirming PPL is a poor proxy for reasoning at moderate sparsity.

\noindent\textbf{Capacity-limited ($\geq$50\%).}
Global/uniform gap vanishes (MMLU 27.89\% vs.\ 27.77\%, both $\approx$ random)---model capacity is insufficient regardless of allocation. % exp_id: bench_kl50_global, d030_kl50_uniform
We recommend 30\% sparsity for downstream fidelity.
We note that the exact sparsity boundaries (30\%/40\%/50\%) are observed on LLaMA-3.1-8B and may shift with model scale or architecture.


\subsection{SPON Orthogonality Analysis}
\label{sec:spon}

We decompose the output perturbation at 50\% sparsity to analyze SPON's limited contribution.
The predictor's effect on the hidden state has L2 norm 482.65, while SPON's additive correction has norm 76.2 (15.8\% of the predictor's magnitude) with cosine similarity 0.196, confirming near-orthogonality. % exp_id: spon_decomposition
Under the \texttt{evaluate.py} protocol, SPON provides 2.4\% improvement under TEAL-path inference (12.08 $\to$ 11.79) and 7.3\% under hard-mask inference (9.22 $\to$ 8.99). % exp_id: spon_2048_s42, mvp_kl_norm_v2_s42
The KL predictor already captures most of the recoverable signal, leaving little residual for static bias correction.


\paragraph{Cross-Architecture Evaluation.}
As a preliminary evaluation on one additional architecture, we apply the same training procedure to Mistral-7B-v0.3~\cite{jiang2023mistral} (GQA + sliding window attention) at 50\% sparsity.
The KL predictor achieves PPL 8.98 (dense: 5.52), a +63\% relative degradation compared to +93\% on LLaMA (dense 6.12 $\to$ 11.79), suggesting the approach may generalize to other SwiGLU architectures, though broader evaluation is needed. % exp_id: kl_50_mistral, bench_kl50_global


\paragraph{Inference Efficiency.}
The KL-sparse pipeline adds 6.4\% overhead to dense inference (predictor 2.7\% + mask indexing 3.7\%), while eliminating the compensation network saves $\sim$2.7\% latency and 67.2M parameters.
Full benchmarks are reported in Appendix~\ref{app:latency}.

\paragraph{Training Stability.}
Three-seed evaluation (50\% uniform) yields PPL $11.92 \pm 0.07$ (CV$<$1\%), confirming low sensitivity to initialization; details in Appendix~\ref{app:stability}.

